      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(google-meet): bound JSON response body reads to prevent OOM",
        "body": "## Summary\n\nReplace raw `response.json()` with `readProviderJsonResponse` (16 MiB cap) across the google-meet extension to prevent unbounded memory allocation from oversized HTTP response bodies. The fix covers 5 call sites across 3 files:\n\n- `meet.ts`: `fetchGoogleMeetJson`, `fetchGoogleMeetSpace`, `createGoogleMeetSpace`\n- `calendar.ts`: `fetchGoogleCalendarEvents`\n- `oauth.ts`: `executeGoogleTokenRequest`\n\nAlso extracted the duplicated `hasConfig` check in `createGoogleMeetSpace` (the same `params.config && Object.keys(params.config).length > 0` was evaluated twice).\n\n## Real behavior proof\n\nThe vulnerability: an attacker-controlled or misconfigured Google API endpoint could return a multi-gigabyte JSON response body. `response.json()` materializes the full body in memory, causing OOM.\n\n`readProviderJsonResponse` reads the response body with a 16 MiB cap (via `readResponseWithLimit`), preventing unbounded allocation. Existing error-path reads in `google-api-errors.ts` were already bounded at 8 KiB via `readResponseTextLimited`.\n\n## Tests\n\n- New `error-body-boundary.test.ts`: loopback HTTP server test with 4 MiB oversized response body + valid body passthrough\n- All 180 existing google-meet tests pass (14 files, +2 new)\n\n```bash\n# All tests pass\n$ pnpm test extensions/google-meet/\nTest Files  14 passed (14)\nTests  180 passed (180)\n```",
        "branch": "fix/google-meet-bound-json-response-reads",
